  {
   "id": 41,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    40,
    -700
   ],
   "size": [
    980,
    640
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 41,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "## Viggle-Animate: put a character from a picture into a video\n\n1. **Load Video**: the driving clip, read at 24 fps. Examples: `talking_kitchen.mp4`, `talking_bench.mp4`,\n   `presenter.mp4` (with speech), `dance_studio.mp4`, `wave_street.mp4`, or upload your own.\n2. **Load Image**: who or what to put in: a photo, a drawing, an animal. Examples: `knight.jpg`, `robot.jpg`,\n   `wizard.jpg`, `pirate.jpg`, `fox.jpg`, `gingerbread.jpg`.\n3. **Queue.** Step 1, the clip's first frame with that character painted in, appears in *Save Image* (the\n   editor's description of your image is in *Preview Any*). If it is not right, add a sentence in the extra\n   instructions box (the empty text box) or change the seed and queue again: only the changed parts re-run.\n4. Step 2, Viggle-Animate, renders the whole clip in 124-frame windows. The clip's soundtrack is held in the model\n   as clean audio, so mouths follow the speech (lip-sync, as the model card's `sample.py --audio`).\n\nThe clip is scaled to about 0.4 MP, the size Viggle-Animate was trained at. With the models loaded a 5 s clip\ntakes about a minute on an RTX PRO 6000; loading takes a minute or two the first time. Identity or light can\ndrift between windows on long clips. If this ComfyUI is shared, everyone using it can see what is uploaded and rendered, and jobs run one at a time in one queue. Only use footage of people who agreed to it, and label the results as AI-generated when you publish them. Results are in `output/viggle/` and `output/ripple/`."
   ],
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  }
